.TH CHTTP_DEFAULT_CLIENT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttp_default_client \- give the default HTTP client of the process
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

chttpcli chttp_default_client(void);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function returns the process's default client, which the library
creates the first time it is needed. The function is thread-safe, and
a process never gets more than one default client.

The default client has the default settings: pool_size is the CPU
count, there are no timeouts and TLS verification is on. You can
change them with the same chttpclient_set_* functions as for any other
client.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The handle of the default client, or CHTTPCLI_INVALID if the
initialization fails.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Do not pass this handle to chttpclient_destroy. The module owns the
default client and destroys it at process exit, from a handler that it
registers with atexit(3) when it first builds the client. A call to
chttp_default_client from an exit handler that runs after that one
returns CHTTPCLI_INVALID.
If you destroy it anyway, the chttpclient_destroy call does not crash,
because the module recognizes the handle and clears its own reference
to it. The library never builds the default client again, though, so
every later call to chttp_default_client, chttp_do, chttp_get and the
other wrappers fails cleanly and runs no request, for the rest of the
process's life. If you need a client whose lifetime you control,
create your own with chttpclient_create or chttpclient_create_mp.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpclient_create (3),
.BR chttpclient_destroy (3),
.BR chttp_do (3),
.BR chttp_get (3)
